Public investment did not expand because theory matured in isolation. It expanded when history exposed the insufficiency of prevailing geometries. The evolution of public investment science can be read as a sequence of responses to crisis-social crises that destabilised practice and epistemic crises that destabilised understanding.&nbsp;
<div>
 The earliest geometry was Euclidean. Infrastructure was additive accumulation in space. Feasibility was the invariant. Yet time curved that geometry. Discounting introduced intertemporal valuation. Scarcity introduced optimisation within constrained polytopes. Debt crises revealed multiplicative fiscal dynamics. Contractual failure revealed strategic behaviour under asymmetric information. Institutional fragility revealed transformation capacity as a state variable. Climate risk has now introduced viability constraints under correlated and cascading shocks.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Each enlargement was compelled. Social crises-world war, financial collapse, systemic climate shocksforced policymakers to reach for mathematics that already existed but had not been operationalised. Epistemic crises run deeper. The discovery of √2 revealed that stretch exceeds rational closure. The introduction of √-1 revealed rotation as a new operator. These moments altered the structure of mathematics itself. Nature permits only stretch and rotation. Human understanding advances when crisis exposes the limits of our representation of those two operations.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Public investment now confronts both forms of crisis simultaneously. Social perturbations demand deeper mathematical engagement. Yet the complexity of coupled systems challenges the closure assumptions embedded in existing mathematical foundations. Domains evolve. Interaction terms dominate. Safe sets move. Structure becomes historical rather than fixed. The modern public investment state can be represented as motion on a high-dimensional manifold whose coordinates were accumulated historically. Complexity is not confusion. It is layered structure under interaction. The central epistemic requirement is humility: to know enough to recognise what is unknown, and to govern within evolving geometry rather than fixed design.
</div>
